```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>",
                      fig.path = "figures/ratones-",
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 5, dpi = 80,
                      out.width = "100%")
```

`ratones_morpho` ships with the package (Penna et al. 2017): 35 cranial
distances for 329 mice from five artificial selection lines, with sex, age,
generation and line. This vignette fits four of the distances, with the
relationship matrix built by `animal_design()` from the dam and sire recorded on
each mouse, and then fits the same model in size and shape coordinates.

```{r packages, message = FALSE}
library(stanimal)
library(patchwork)
```

## Data

```{r data}
dat <- ratones_morpho
table(dat$selection)
```

Four distances spanning the skull, on the log scale as usual for morphometrics.
The 35 distances are moderately integrated (one principal component explains
about 40% of the variance), so a handful of them already carry a correlated
genetic signal.

```{r traits}
traits <- c("IS_PM", "NSL_NA", "PT_BA", "BA_OPI")
dat[traits] <- log(dat[traits])
round(cor(dat[traits]), 2)
```

`animal_design()` builds `Y`, `X` and `A` in one call. The formula gives the
fixed effects: sex, age at death and selection line (the lines differ in body
weight by construction, so their means must be modelled). `pedigree = ped` adds
the relationship matrix. The rows of `ratones_morpho` are named with the
individual IDs, so the pedigree is matched to the rows of `Y` automatically, and
parents without records are added as founders.

```{r design}
ped <- ratones_pedigree
design <- suppressWarnings(
  animal_design(cbind(IS_PM, NSL_NA, PT_BA, BA_OPI) ~ SEX + AGE + LIN,
                dat, pedigree = ped))
dim(design$Y)
colnames(design$X)
dim(design$A)
```

## Fit

```{r fit, message = FALSE}
fit <- lmm_animal(design$Y, design$X, design$A, lkj_prior = 2,
                  iter = 3000, warmup = 1500, chains = 4, cores = 4,
                  refresh = 0, seed = 1)
fit$summary(c("h2", "sigma_G", "sigma_E", "corrG"))
fit$diagnostic_summary()
```

The fixed effects, named after the columns of `X`:

```{r beta}
beta_draws <- rename_beta(fit, "beta")
posterior::summarise_draws(beta_draws)
```

The traits are log distances, so variances are small numbers; heritabilities and
correlations are scale free and easier to read.

```{r matrices}
signif(draws_mean(fit, "G"), 2)
round(draws_mean(fit, "corrG"), 2)
```

`P = G + E` is estimated after the fixed effects, so it is smaller than the raw
covariance of the traits, which also contains the sex, age and line effects. It
is close to the covariance of the least-squares residuals:

```{r P}
signif(draws_mean(fit, "P"), 2)
signif(cov(residuals(lm(cbind(IS_PM, NSL_NA, PT_BA, BA_OPI) ~ SEX + AGE + LIN,
                        dat))), 2)
```

## Plots

Where the prior and the posterior overlap for h2 and `corrG`, the data add
little information.

```{r prior-posterior, fig.height = 6}
plot_prior_posterior(fit, trait_names = traits, ncol = 4)
```

```{r forest, fig.height = 5}
plot_forest(fit, trait_names = traits)
```

The residuals after the fixed effects, with posterior draws of the `G` ellipses
and the genetic correlations. `X = design$X` removes the sex, age and line
effects, so the cloud and the dashed ellipse have roughly the covariance
`P = G + E`.

```{r ellipses, fig.height = 7}
plot_G_ellipses(fit, design$Y, trait_names = traits, n_show = 100,
                X = design$X)
```

```{r corr, fig.height = 4}
plot_corr(draws_mean(fit, "corrG"), "Posterior mean corrG") +
  plot_corr(cor(dat[traits]), "Observed P correlations")
```

## The same model in size and shape coordinates

Size plus the three shape axes span the same space as the four traits, so this
is the same model in another parametrisation: same number of traits, same `X`
and `A`. The prior is not invariant, though: the LKJ prior now acts on
correlations between size and shape rather than between the raw traits, and the
shape axes are principal components, so their correlations are expected to be
small. `lkj_prior` stays at 2 to keep the two fits comparable.

```{r size-shape, message = FALSE}
ss <- size_shape(dat[traits], n_shape = length(traits) - 1L)
dat_ss <- cbind(dat, ss$scores)
design_ss <- suppressWarnings(
  animal_design(cbind(size, shape1, shape2, shape3) ~ SEX + AGE + LIN,
                dat_ss, pedigree = ped))
fit_ss <- lmm_animal(design_ss$Y, design_ss$X, design_ss$A, lkj_prior = 2,
                     iter = 3000, warmup = 1500, chains = 4, cores = 4,
                     refresh = 0, seed = 1)
```

`back_transform()` maps every draw back to the measured traits, returning `G` as
a traits by traits by draws array named after the traits.

```{r back-transform}
post_ss <- back_transform(fit_ss, ss)
G_traits <- draws_mean(fit, "G")
G_size_shape <- draws_mean(post_ss, "G")
round(G_traits, 4)
round(G_size_shape, 4)
# largest difference, relative to the largest element of G
round(max(abs(G_traits - G_size_shape)) / max(abs(G_traits)), 3)
```

The two fits are to linear maps of the same data, so `fit_loo()` can compare them
on the trait scale once it is told the transformation:

```{r loo, warning = FALSE}
loo::loo_compare(list(raw = fit_loo(fit),
                      size_shape = fit_loo(fit_ss, transform = ss)))
```

The difference is well inside two standard errors, so the data do not prefer
one parametrisation: they are the same model under slightly different priors.
